# BK-2 off-server workstation recovery (rule 230)

**Status:** ACTIVE from `DMS-BK2-BACKUP-RELIABILITY-WSL2-RECOVERY-1` (2026-11-14). Sprint record:
`docs/sprints/dms-bk2-backup-reliability-wsl2-recovery-1.md`. Operator procedure:
`docs/runbooks/bk2-offserver-workstation-recovery.md`. Supersedes nothing; it refines how the workstation half of BK-2
reports itself. The server half (`dms-bk2-status`, its ack ledger and thresholds) is unchanged.

## 1. The defect

The BK-2 pull runs on the owner's Windows 11 + WSL2 workstation (`dms-bk2-pull.timer`, every 30 minutes) and copies
the server's encrypted packages onto an encrypted backup HDD. `wsl --mount` does not survive a Windows or WSL restart,
so after every restart the HDD is detached until the custodian re-attaches and unlocks it. On 2026-11-13/14 that went
unnoticed for hours (the 8D deploy preflight stopped on it):

- a disk that was simply not attached was reported as `IDENTITY_MISMATCH`, the same word as a foreign device;
- nothing alerted: desktop notifications were disabled in the system unit and no desktop session exists there;
- the pull exited 0 when the HDD was unavailable, so systemd recorded every run as a success;
- the local status copied `OFF_SERVER` from the server's ack ledger, so it could read `OK` with the HDD detached;
- nothing proved that a custodian's mount was visible to PID 1, the namespace the timer is cloned from;
- the custodian procedure (elevated attach, unlock, mount) existed only as habit.

## 2. Where it lives

The tooling is in the owner's **private** BK-2 tree on the workstation (the directory `ExecStart` of
`dms-bk2-pull.service` names): `bin/` (pull, identity check, status, custodian helper), `etc/bk2.conf` (identity and
thresholds), `tests/run-tests.sh`, and `releases/` (rollback snapshots). The HDD's serial, WWN, LUKS and ext4 UUIDs, the
pull account and the key-escrow paths stay there. This repository is public: it carries only the invariants below and
the procedure, never those values. The guard test fails on the shapes of the hardware identifiers. The account and
escrow names are kept out by review, because a guard would have to publish them in order to detect them.

## 3. Identity chain and states

`hdd-identity-check.sh` is read-only and never unlocks or mounts. Its last line is always `HDD_STATE=<state>`, and every
non-OK state is preceded by a `REASON:` line. Callers cross-check the state against the exit code; disagreement is
`UNVERIFIABLE`.

| State | Exit | Meaning | Who acts |
|---|---|---|---|
| `OK` | 0 | every link proven, including the mount as PID 1 sees it | nobody |
| `ABSENT` | 2 | the disk is not attached to WSL (a leftover mapping or mount without it is `MISMATCH`) | custodian |
| `LOCKED` | 2 | disk and LUKS2 partition proven; the canonical mapping is not open | custodian |
| `NOT_MOUNTED` | 2 | mapping and ext4 proven; nothing mounted | custodian |
| `NOT_IN_PID1_NS` | 2 | mounted only in the caller's mount namespace; the timer cannot see it | custodian, manually |
| `MISMATCH` | 3 | any link differs: model, serial, WWN, LUKS UUID or parent disk, dm uuid, slaves, ext4 UUID, device, fstype, bind of a subdirectory | stop |
| `WRONG_MAPPING` | 3 | the LUKS partition is open under another name or has an extra holder | stop |
| `DUPLICATE_MOUNT` | 3 | stacked mounts on the mount point, or the filesystem also mounted elsewhere | stop |
| `READ_ONLY` | 3 | mount or superblock is read-only (possible filesystem error); never repaired automatically | stop |
| `UNVERIFIABLE` | 4 | configuration missing, or PID 1's mount table unreadable | stop |

## 4. PID 1's mount namespace

The mount is judged from `/proc/1/mountinfo` (world-readable), never from the caller's own table, because every
systemd service starts from PID 1's namespace. The custodian helper mounts with `nsenter --target 1 --mount`, and the
identity check then proves PID 1 sees exactly one read-write ext4 mount of the filesystem root at the mount point.

## 5. Custodian unlock

`bk2-custodian.sh attach` is the only recovery path, and only the custodian runs it:

- it refuses without a terminal on stdin and stdout, and refuses to run as root (sudo is per step);
- the LUKS passphrase is typed into cryptsetup's own prompt: no key file, no passphrase variable, nothing piped into
  cryptsetup, no recovery key in any script;
- every privileged step runs only after the identity check has proven the chain up to that step, the check runs again
  after each step, and a step that makes no progress stops the procedure;
- the Windows step runs elevated through UAC and finds the disk by serial and model again right before
  `wsl --mount <disk> --bare`; more or fewer than one match attaches nothing;
- it never formats, initialises, repairs or wipes anything;
- it is idempotent: it does only the missing steps, then starts one pull and prints the status.

`bk2-custodian.sh plan` and `windows-command` are read-only and show the exact commands.

## 6. Result semantics

A pull run writes `result=` and exits accordingly; neither ever claims more than that run verified.

| Result | Exit | When |
|---|---|---|
| `OK` | 0 | server reachable, HDD `OK`, every listed package confirmed on the HDD this run, server `ON_SERVER=OK` and `OFF_SERVER=OK` |
| `DEGRADED` | 75 | nothing failed, but the copy could not be confirmed: HDD unavailable, server unreachable, packages pending, server `WARN`. The unit shows failed. |
| `FAIL` | 1 | identity in doubt, transfer or verification failure, an invalid HDD copy, server `FAIL` |

The local `OFF_SERVER` is `OK` only under the `OK` conditions. Otherwise it is `UNVERIFIED` (HDD not available), `UNKNOWN`
(server unreachable), `FAIL`, or the server's pending state. `server_OFF_SERVER` keeps the server's ack ledger next to
it for reference. Acknowledgement rules are unchanged: a package is acked only after size and SHA-256 against the
manifest, an O_DIRECT re-read, decryption through the escrow chain compared with the server's plaintext hashes, and the
documents check.

## 7. Delivered copies are re-confirmed

Every run with the HDD available re-checks each listed package already on it: the `HDD_VERIFIED` record names the
package, the manifest hash equals the server's list, and every file is present at its manifest size. At most every 24
hours (and always before a re-ack), every listed package is re-hashed with O_DIRECT. A copy that fails is never
counted, never re-acked, and raises `HDD_COPY_INVALID`.

## 8. Alerts and SLA

Each run writes `level=` (`none`, `warn` or `crit`) and `local_alerts=`, and logs `BK2_ALERT` to the journal at
priority warning or crit (`journalctl -p warning -u dms-bk2-pull.service`).

| Alert | Level |
|---|---|
| HDD `ABSENT`/`LOCKED`/`NOT_MOUNTED`/`NOT_IN_PID1_NS` | warn, crit after 45 minutes since the last run that saw it `OK` |
| HDD identity fail state | crit |
| oldest pending package older than 26 hours (the server's `DELAYED` threshold) or 72 hours (`STALE`) | crit |
| server unreachable | warn, crit from the third consecutive run |
| sync or verification failure, invalid HDD copy, destination not writable, server `FAIL` | crit |
| server `WARN` or server alerts | warn |

`bk2-status.sh` adds what a run cannot report about itself: a live identity check (so a restart is visible before the
first timer run), the age of the last run (warn after 75 minutes, crit after 225 minutes while the timer is enabled),
a disabled or inactive timer (crit), a run in progress, and leftovers (abandoned `.incoming-*` packages, stray BK-2
gpg-agents, BK-2 temp directories). `--brief` prints nothing when all is well; that suits an interactive-shell banner.

## 9. Deploy preflight

`docs/runbooks/production-host-identity.md` §6b step 3 is unchanged as the gate: the host `dms-bk2-status` must report
`ON_SERVER=OK` and `OFF_SERVER=OK`. Read the workstation verdict early as well. A detached HDD is reported to the owner
before the preflight, because the deploy's own package will wait on the server. A fail state (`MISMATCH`,
`WRONG_MAPPING`, `DUPLICATE_MOUNT`, `READ_ONLY`, `UNVERIFIABLE`) or a `FAIL` result is a HOLD until the owner decides.

## 10. Never

- Never store, print, pass or ask for the LUKS passphrase or a recovery key, in a script, a file, a log, a doc or a chat.
- Never let an automated session unlock or attach the HDD; `attach` refuses without a terminal.
- Never format, initialise, repair (`fsck`) or wipe the backup HDD, and never delete a verified package.
- Never `wsl --mount` a disk chosen by number alone; the serial decides.
- Never hand-edit `status.env`, and never treat a run's exit code or systemd's "success" as the verdict.
- Never put the HDD identifiers, the pull account or the escrow paths into this repository.
